   "source": [
    "## 2. Y transformation\n",
    "\n",
    "**This is the decisive step for Function 1, and it was not obvious.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The first version of this model used an RBF kernel with a fixed length-scale of\n",
    "0.1 and `alpha=1e-10` — effectively assuming noise-free observations. It found\n",
    "nothing. The natural response was to blame the kernel, so it was revised:\n",
    "Matérn instead of RBF, a learnable length-scale instead of a fixed one, a\n",
    "WhiteKernel instead of a fixed alpha.\n",
    "\n",
    "It still found nothing.\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw values explain why. They span about 180 orders of magnitude, from\n",
    "1e-192 down to 1e-248 and up to 1e-3. A GP compares observations by\n",
    "subtracting them, so at that spread the kernel length-scale has no coherent\n",
    "interpretation and the optimiser stalls. Nearly every observation reads as\n",
    "zero, and there is no structure in a column of zeros.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The problem was the scale of the data, not the model.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The transform is `signed_log10(y) = sign(y) · log10(|y| + ε)`. It compresses\n",
    "the range onto something the kernel can work with, while preserving the sign —\n",
    "which matters here, because the two negative readings carry the strongest\n",
    "signal.\n",
    "\n",
    "The GP is fitted on the transformed values and the acquisition function\n",
    "operates on the same scale. `Y_raw` is kept only for reporting."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "66ca422a",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.233147Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.232848Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.239238Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.238568Z"
    }
