   "source": [
    "## 3. Kernel\n",
    "\n",
    "`ConstantKernel × Matérn(ν=5/2) + WhiteKernel`\n",
    "\n",
    "| Component | Setting | Role |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `ConstantKernel` | bounds (1e-3, 1e3) | Learns overall signal amplitude. Important because the transformed values still span −2 to −248. |\n",
    "| `Matérn(ν=2.5)` | — | Sharp but continuous peaks near sources. RBF assumes infinite smoothness, which over-smooths a surface this sparse. |\n",
    "| `length_scale` | 0.05, bounds (1e-5, 0.5) | Very short. The signal is concentrated in a small region rather than spread smoothly. |\n",
    "| `WhiteKernel` | 1e-4, bounds (1e-8, 1e-1) | Explicit noise term, replacing the original `alpha=1e-10`. Keeps the fit numerically stable. |\n",
    "\n",
    "The fitted length-scale is printed below. If it settles on its lower bound, the\n",
    "model is signalling that it wants to vary faster than the kernel permits."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "4a74758c",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.240626Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.240496Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.481888Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-18T11:36:57.481208Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Optimised kernel:\n",
      "  1.18**2 * Matern(length_scale=0.293, nu=2.5) + WhiteKernel(noise_level=1e-08)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "#  KERNEL\n",
    "# -----------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "kernel = (\n",
    "    ConstantKernel(\n",
    "        constant_value=1.0,\n",
    "        constant_value_bounds=(1e-3, 1e3),      # overall signal amplitude\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    * Matern(\n",
    "        nu=2.5,                                 # sharp but continuous peaks\n",
    "        length_scale=0.05,\n",
    "        length_scale_bounds=(1e-5, 0.5),\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    + WhiteKernel(\n",
    "        noise_level=1e-4,                       # measurement noise\n",
    "        noise_level_bounds=(1e-8, 1e-1),\n",
    "    )\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# -----------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "#  GP MODEL (fitted on transformed Y)\n",
    "#\n",
    "#  n_restarts_optimizer=25 : fitting the kernel is itself a non-convex\n",
    "#      search that readily gets stuck, so we try 25 starting points.\n",
    "#  normalize_y=True        : centres and scales the target before fitting.\n",
    "# -----------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "beta = 1.96          # UCB exploration-exploitation trade-off\n",
    "\n",
    "model = GaussianProcessRegressor(\n",
    "    kernel=kernel,\n",
    "    n_restarts_optimizer=25,\n",
    "    normalize_y=True,\n",
    "    random_state=42,\n",
    ")\n",
    "model.fit(X, Y)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Optimised kernel:\\n  {model.kernel_}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fitted_ls = model.kernel_.k1.k2.length_scale\n",
    "if fitted_ls <= 1e-5 * 1.1:\n",
    "    print(f\"\\n  WARNING: length_scale {fitted_ls:.2e} is at its lower bound.\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  The model wants sharper variation than the kernel allows.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8e646a0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Does the model actually work?\n",
    "\n",
    "Fitting a GP always produces a smooth, plausible-looking surface. That is not\n",
    "evidence it has learned anything.\n",
    "\n",
    "Leave-one-out cross-validation tests it directly: hide one observation, refit\n",
    "on the rest, predict the hidden point, repeat for all 15.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two measures are reported.\n",
    "\n",
    "**RMSE** is the average size of the prediction error, in the units of this\n",
    "function's target. It is an absolute measure — it says how wrong the model is,\n",
    "not merely how it compares with an alternative — which makes it the more\n",
    "interpretable of the two and the better choice when comparing different kinds\n",
    "of model.\n",
    "\n",
    "**R²** expresses the same error as a proportion of the variance: 1.0 is\n",
    "perfect, 0.0 is no better than always predicting the average, and below 0.0 is\n",
    "worse than that.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both are shown, along with the RMSE of simply predicting the mean, which is the\n",
    "benchmark any useful model has to beat. Whatever these return applies to every\n",
    "suggestion the model makes below."
   ]
  },
